   <h1>Amarantine (2005)</h1>

<a class="new-amazon-album-link" href="https://amzn.to/3SUVkgw" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">
  <Image src={am} alt="Amarantine (2005)" width="300" height="300" /></a>

<p>Enya's mesmerizing <strong>sixth studio album</strong>, <strong><em>Amarantine</em></strong>, made its official debut on <strong>November 21, 2005</strong> in Europe and <strong>November 22, 2005</strong> in North America.</p>

<p>Recognizing its outstanding artistry, the record was honored with the prestigious <strong>Grammy Award for Best New Age Album in 2007</strong>, marking Enya's fourth career victory in that category.</p>

<h2>A New Linguistic Path</h2>

<p>Within the enchanting tapestry of <em>Amarantine</em>, a unique creative evolution unfolds across its 45-minute runtime.</p>

<p>While <strong>Irish Gaelic</strong> takes a backseat for the first time on an Enya album, she and lyricist <strong>Roma Ryan</strong> ventured into uncharted territory by weaving three tracks in an invented language known as <strong>Loxian</strong>.</p>

<h2>The Creation of Loxian</h2>

<p>Described by Enya and Roma Ryan as a futuristic language created for spacefarers on a distant planet, Loxian adds an ethereal touch to the album's lyrical landscape.</p>

<p>This ambitious endeavor grew out of their prior experiences crafting lyrics in <strong>J. R. R. Tolkien's Elvish languages</strong>, such as <strong>"May it Be"</strong> and <strong>"Aníron"</strong> for <em>The Lord of the Rings</em>.</p>

<p><a href="https://amzn.to/3SUVkgw" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Stream or download Enya songs</a></p>

<h2>Production and Recording</h2>

<p>Following the release of her comprehensive compilation box set <strong><em>Only Time – The Collection</em> (2002)</strong>, Enya took a brief hiatus before commencing work on new material in <strong>September 2003</strong>.</p>

<p>This project marked her first full-length studio release since her massive commercial success with <strong><em>A Day Without Rain</em> (2000)</strong>.</p>

<p>The album was recorded in Ireland alongside her longtime collaborators, producer and arranger <strong>Nicky Ryan</strong> and lyricist <strong>Roma Ryan</strong>.</p>

<p>Production took place at <strong>Aigle Studio</strong>, located inside the Ryans' home in <strong>Killiney, County Dublin</strong>, where Enya followed a strict schedule working from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. on weekdays.</p>

<p>The completed album was dedicated to the memory of <strong>BBC producer Tony McAuley</strong>, who originally commissioned Enya to write the soundtrack for <em>The Celts</em> in 1986.</p>

<h2>Announcement and Leaks</h2>

<p>The official release date was first announced by Roma Ryan on <strong>September 23, 2005</strong>, via Enya's official forum.</p>

<p>Despite strict security measures around the project, the album's audio content was leaked to the Internet on <strong>November 11, 2005</strong>.</p>

<p>Promotional photography for the album was shot by photographer <strong>Simon Fowler</strong> over two days in <strong>June 2005</strong> at <strong>Knebworth House</strong> in Hertfordshire, England.</p>

<h2>Visuals and Launch Event</h2>

<p>The surreal music video for the title single <strong>"Amarantine"</strong> was directed by <strong>Tim Royes</strong>.</p>

<p>The enchanting forest through which Enya walks was crafted by digital matte painter <strong>Charles Darby</strong>, renowned for his visual work on <em>The Fifth Element</em>, <em>Titanic</em>, and <em>The Matrix</em>.</p>

<p>The album was officially launched on <strong>November 27, 2005</strong>, at the historic <strong>Castle of Vaux-le-Vicomte</strong> in France.</p>

<p>Record label <strong>Warner Music</strong> hosted a lavish banquet where Enya wore a striking red gown designed by <strong>Maria Grachvogel</strong>, capped by a grand fireworks display synchronized to the new music.</p>

