<h2>Special Christmas Edition</h2>

<p>In <strong>November 2006</strong>, an expanded version titled <strong><em>Amarantine - Special Christmas Edition</em></strong> was released worldwide.</p>

<p>This re-release featured a bonus disc containing four holiday-themed tracks: <strong>"Adeste, Fideles"</strong>, <strong>"The Magic of the Night"</strong>, <strong>"We Wish You a Merry Christmas"</strong>, and <strong>"Christmas Secrets"</strong>.</p>

<h2>Loxian Language Origins</h2>

<p>The decision to invent <strong>Loxian</strong> arose when Roma Ryan struggled to write satisfying lyrics in English, Irish, or Latin for the track <strong>"Water Shows the Hidden Heart"</strong>.</p>

<p>The phonetic foundation of the language was directly derived from the wordless vocal sounds sung by Enya during initial melody rehearsals at the studio keyboard.</p>

<p>The three Loxian tracks featured on the album are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><a href="#Less_than_a_pearl"><strong>Less Than A Pearl</strong> - Heah Viiya</a></li>
  <li><a href="#The_River_Sings"><strong>The River Sings</strong> - Ea Hymm Llay Hey</a></li>
  <li><a href="#Water_shows_the_hidden_heart"><strong>Water Shows The Hidden Heart</strong> - Syoombrraya</a></li>
</ul>

<p>Explaining the concept behind the language, Enya noted that the Loxians represent a culture living in space, gazing out into the cosmos and questioning whether other life exists.</p>

<h2>Companion Book Release</h2>

<p>On <strong>December 5, 2005</strong>, Roma Ryan published a companion book titled <strong><em>Water Shows the Hidden Heart</em></strong>, detailing the mythology, poetry, and character scripts of the Loxian culture.</p>

<p>A second edition of this book was later bundled into the <strong>Deluxe Collector's Edition</strong> box set of <em>Amarantine</em> in late 2006.</p>

<h2>Japanese Commercial Tie-Ins</h2>

<p>In <strong>September 2004</strong>, the track <strong>"Sumiregusa (Wild Violet)"</strong> was premiered in Japan to support a major corporate advertising campaign for <strong>Panasonic</strong>.</p>

<p>The song featured prominently across national Japanese television broadcasts promoting Panasonic's <strong>"Viera"</strong> flat-screen televisions and <strong>"Diga"</strong> DVD recorders.</p>

<p>The opening bars of "Sumiregusa" were adapted from an arrangement of the classic hymn <strong>"Hark! The Vesper Hymn Is Stealing"</strong> by Irish composer <strong>John Andrew Stevenson</strong>.</p>

<p>Enya has stated in interviews that title track <strong>"Amarantine"</strong> and <strong>"If I Could Be Where You Are"</strong> remain her personal favorite pieces from this album.</p>

